390625, -0.024017333984375, 0.005725860595703125, 0.0302581787109375, -0.008392333984375, -0.0015840530395507812, 0.0139617919921875, -0.01517486572265625, -0.0121002197265625, 0.036468505859375, 0.00846099853515625, 0.044677734375, -0.00537109375, 0.0128021240234375, -0.0196990966796875, 0.0322265625, 0.0158538818359375, 0.00641632080078125, 0.0026836395263671875, -0.0262603759765625, -0.01007080078125, 0.00395965576171875, 0.0025348663330078125, -0.0136566162109375, 0.03289794921875, -3.5822391510009766e-05, 0.03448486328125, -0.0173187255859375, 0.006702423095703125, -0.010711669921875, 0.004871368408203125, 0.0212554931640625, -0.0186767578125, 0.00952911376953125, -0.00463104248046875, -0.0194091796875, 0.031280517578125, -0.024139404296875, -0.027130126953125, 0.022857666015625, 0.00324249267578125, 0.010009765625, 0.005405426025390625, 0.0335693359375, 0.0430908203125, -0.0164642333984375, -0.01136016845703125, -0.0021114349365234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods  \n\nStudy Design and Data  \nThis retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline Architecture  \nThe system comprised two primary agent types orchestrated in a sequential pipeline. First, a rule-based agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections (e.g., lungs, liver, kidneys). The regex patterns were developed iteratively based on a sample of local reports and covered common anatomical terms and section headers. Second, a local large language model (LLM) agent\u2014specifically, a 7B-parameter Llama-2 model running on-premises\u2014was used to resolve ambiguous or complex sentence assignments and to perform semantic checks. The LLM was not fine-tuned on local data but was prompted with institution-specific instructions to maximize compatibility with local reporting styles. The agents interacted via a controller script: the regex agent parsed the report and generated a preliminary section assignment, which was then reviewed and refined by the LLM agent. The LLM also performed quality assurance (QA) checks on the structured output. This hybrid approach leveraged the precision of rule-based parsing for well-structured content and the flexibility of LLMs for handling variability in reporting language.\n\nQuality Assurance Tasks  \nThe system flagged reports for three predefined QA tasks: (a) mismatches between Findings and Impression sections or within sections (e.g., findings present in one section but not reflected in the Impression), (b) gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients), and (c) undocumented communication of critical findings (e.g., critical results not documented as communicated). Each QA task was implemented as a separate module within the LLM agent, using prompt-based logic to identify relevant discrepancies.\n\nEvaluation Design and Metrics  \nA random subset of 45 reports was selected for radiologist evaluation. The sample size was chosen to provide a 95% confidence interval of \u00b115% for the primary outcome (correct restructuring rate), assuming an expected accuracy of 70%. Stratification ensured balanced representation: 23 flagged reports (across all QA tasks) and 22 unflagged reports were randomly sampled to reflect the overall flagged/unflagged ratio. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed each report and assessed: (1) correctness of restructuring (defined as accurate anatomical sectioning with preservation of original content), (2) QA performance for each flagged issue (rated as true positive, false positive, true negative, or false negative for each QA task), and (3) whether any clinically important information was omitted or fabricated. Disagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic.\n\nResults  \n\nReport Structuring  \nThe system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content. Of the 45 evaluated reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and 12 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had reviewer disagreement. Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring correctness was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement.\n\nQuality Assurance Task Performance  \nAcross the 45 evaluated reports, the system flagged 14 for section mismatches, 2 for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 1 for undocumented critical findings. For section mismatches, sensitivity was 0.83 (10/12 true positives), specificity 0.95 (31/33 true negatives), with 2 false positives and 2 false negatives. For gender-anatomy conflicts, sensitivity and specificity were both 1.0 (2/2 true positives, 43/43 true negatives). For undocumented critical findings, the single flagged case was a true positive. No clinically important information was omitted or fabricated in any case, as agreed by both reviewers.\n\nAnalysis of Flagged Errors  \nOf the 90 reports flagged in the full dataset, 80 (12.5%) were for section mismatches, 7 (1.1%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 3 (0.5%) for undocumented critical findings. Most section mismatches were minor, such as omission of benign findings from the Impression, but 12 involved potentially clinically significant discrepancies (e.g., a pulmonary nodule described in Findings but not mentioned in Impression). Error analysis revealed that false positives were often due to ambiguous language or atypical section headers not recognized by the regex agent, while false negatives occurred when findings were described in non-standard phrasing that the LLM failed to map correctly. Gender-anatomy conflict errors were rare and typically resulted from template errors in dictation. Undocumented critical findings were correctly identified, but the small number limits conclusions about performance.\n\nLimitations  \nThis study is limited by its retrospective design, single-institution data, and potential overfitting to local reporting styles. The use of only board-certified radiologists from the same institution for both report generation and evaluation introduces possible evaluator bias. The sample size for radiologist evaluation was modest and may not capture the full range of reporting variability. The system\u2019s generalizability to other institutions, radiologists, or imaging modalities remains untested, and no external reviewers were included. There was no comparison to existing structuring or QA methods, and the system was not prospectively deployed in clinical workflow. These limitations restrict the interpretation of results and preclude conclusions about real-world clinical impact.\n\nConclusion  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI system combining rule-based and LLM agents enabled radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system demonstrated promising but preliminary performance in a limited, single-institution radiologist evaluation. Further validation\u2014including comparison to existing methods, prospective deployment, and multi-institutional studies with external reviewers\u2014is needed to confirm generalizability and clinical utility. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice, but current findings should be interpreted as preliminary."}}